course: dictionary
lesson: 4255
title: Grassroots
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Grassroots means the ordinary people in a group, not the leaders at the top. Think of grass: it grows from the ground, where everyone can walk on it. The grassroots of a club are the members who play, train and help out.
explain: «At grassroots level playing time is how children improve, and fair minutes keep everyone learning and coming back.» Grassroots level means local clubs where ordinary children play. The clues are children, improve and playing time. Swap in "ordinary local" for grassroots and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «Many grassroots referees are teenagers, and abuse from the touchline is a big reason referees give up.» Here grassroots describes referees who work in local games, not at the top of the sport. The sentence also gives a sad clue: shouting from the side of the pitch makes people quit.
explain: Grassroots can also describe a campaign that starts with ordinary people. A petition to save a park, started by local families, is a grassroots campaign. If leaders say they will listen to the grassroots, they mean the everyday members and local people.
fact: Grassroots means the ordinary people in a group, club or movement, not the leaders.
fact: As an adjective, grassroots describes something that starts locally with ordinary people, such as grassroots football.
fact: Grassroots is also written as grass-roots.
fact: A grassroots campaign is begun by ordinary people, such as a petition to save a park.
fact: «Grassroots» can help you spot who is doing the real work: the ordinary people, not the people in charge.
exercise: «At grassroots level playing time is how children improve.» What does grassroots level mean here? | A type of grass | A school exam | *Local clubs where ordinary children play | The top professional teams
exercise: What kind of word is grassroots in «Many grassroots referees are teenagers»? | A noun, the name of a club | An adverb, meaning very quickly | A verb, meaning to dig | *An adjective, describing the referees
exercise: A group of local people start a petition to save their park. What is this called? | A national television show | *A grassroots campaign | A royal decree | A referee's whistle
test: The grassroots of a club are its | *Ordinary members | Leaders | Owners | Sponsors
test: «The leaders said they would listen to the grassroots.» Who are the grassroots here? | *The ordinary members and local people | The people in power | The referees only | The newspapers
exam: What does «abuse from the touchline is a big reason referees give up» tell us? | It means referees get stronger when they are shouted at | It means the game is shorter | It means the referees enjoy the game more | *Shouting from the side of the pitch makes referees quit
